<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Trabzon</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Trebizond, Trapezus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Trabzon, historically Trebizond, is a port on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> coast of north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>. Founded as the Greek colony of Trapezus, it was the capital of the Empire of Trebizond from 1204 until the Ottoman conquest of 1461, and a major outlet for the caravan trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>. It remained an important Ottoman port and provincial centre.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/trabzon.jpg" alt="Trabzon" width="320" height="239" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Francesco Bini · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Trebisonda,_ex-chiesa_della_panagia_Chrysokephalos,_oggi_moschea_fatih,_esterno_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Shah Ismail I, founder of the Safavid dynasty that overthrew the Aq Qoyunlu, was Uzun Hasan’s grandson through his daughter by the <b>Trebizond</b> princess Theodora Komnene.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aq-qoyunlu">Aq Qoyunlu · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>The Empire of <b>Trebizond</b> was founded by two grandsons of the Byzantine emperor Andronikos I Komnenos, Alexios and David.</blockquote><a href="/empire/empire-of-trebizond">Empire of Trebizond · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 400 BCE, shortly after the Ten Thousand reached <b>Trapezus</b>, the Colchians fought them and were decisively defeated.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-colchis">Kingdom of Colchis · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Beyond its borders the kingdom dominated Shirvan and much of Armenia, and Tamar helped her kinsmen Alexios and David Komnenos seize <b>Trebizond</b> in 1204, founding the Empire of Trebizond.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-georgia">Kingdom of Georgia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Solomon was taken prisoner, escaped to Ottoman territory and died in exile at <b>Trebizond</b> in 1815.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-imereti">Kingdom of Imereti · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Along the coast lay old Greek colonies such as Sinope, Amisos and <b>Trapezus</b>, centres of trade, fishing and shipbuilding, while the interior was a land of villages, fortresses and great temple estates.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-pontus">Kingdom of Pontus · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
